# BSA 557 cc side-valve Sloper (H30-8 / H31-8) with a period open sidecar.
#
# The 1930s outfit: BSA's inclined-engine "Sloper" with an open tourer chair of
# the Watsonian Monarch type. The 1930 catalogue lists 5.57 h.p. H30-8 as the
# basic 557 cc side-valve Sloper: black frame, green petrol tank, 5 1/2 in front
# brake, 19 x 300 tyres; H30-10 is the de Luxe version with 7 in brakes and
# footboards. For 1931 the equivalent is H31-8. BSA offered eight sidecar models
# in 1930 and four in 1931.
# https://www.bsa1930s.nl/1930.htm
# https://www.bsa1930s.nl/1931.htm
#
# The Sloper's defining shape is the forward-inclined side-valve engine in a
# flat-to-saddle-tank frame, with a sprung saddle and a girder fork. The modelled
# machine is the 1930/31 557 cc version in the catalogued green-tank finish.
#
# Sidecar: a period open tourer with a low rounded nose, open cockpit and folding
# screen, as in the period photograph of a 1930 BSA combination (chair make not
# identified). The Watsonian Monarch was the equivalent open tourer of the
# period; the shape is taken from the photograph, not from a later brochure.
#
# Dimensions: overall about 2.05 m long x 1.40 m wide x 1.15 m high, measured
# from the reference photographs; motorcycle wheelbase about 1.40 m; wheels
# 19 in with 3.00 in section (about 0.72 m overall), per the 1930 catalogue.
#
# Colours: black frame with a green petrol tank is the catalogued 1930 finish for
# the side-valve Slopers; the de Luxe and OHV models used chrome tanks with a
# green top panel. Green and black are the two modelled colourways.
#
# Colour shares and weight: no model-level outfit counts exist for the 1930s.
# Motorcycles remained numerous (690,672 in 1928) while the car parc passed
# them during the decade; the outfit was the characteristic Very-low vehicle of
# the period but was losing ground to cheap cars. Against the 1935 anchor (500)
# the outfit is set at 120 in total: green 75, black 45. Provisional pending the
# central rebase.
#
# Speed 68 km/h (42 mph): a game-balance figure for a 557 cc side-valve outfit.
# Comfort 25/190: sprung saddle and girder fork, no rear suspension, open chair,
# slightly better than the 1920s outfit on better roads.
#
# Dates describe when outfits were seen regularly on UK roads. The Sloper ran
# from 1929 to 1932 in the catalogue; outfits remained in use through the 1930s.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/bsa-sloper-combo.blend in the blends repository.
# References: citycars/references/bsa-sloper-combo/ (gitignored research copies).
# Image credits: 1930 Sloper side view, Yesterdays Antique Motorcycles, CC BY-SA
# 4.0 via Wikimedia Commons; period outfit photograph, Old Classic Car archive
# (R. Jones), research reference only.
# Stage B note: +0.2781 pak units applied after preparation so the motorcycle,
# not the combined bounding box, sits at the tile centre.

obj=citycar
name=BSASloperCombo(Green)
copyright=JamesPetts

DistributionWeight=75
intro_year=1930
intro_month=1
retire_year=1939
retire_month=6
speed=68
class=0,1
maximum_comfortable_journey_minutes=25
maximum_tolerable_journey_minutes=190

